WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.880
Sometimes when running a query, we only want to&nbsp;
see a value once because we may be trying to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.120
how many values of a column are in a table. For&nbsp;
example, I have a very small query set to list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:20.800
product IDs from the Production.ProductCostHistory&nbsp;
table. I will execute it, and we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:28.640
395 rows, but notice that we have many duplicates.&nbsp;
I only want to see each product ID once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.280
The keyword DISTINCT will accomplish&nbsp;
this. I will add the word DISTINCT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:46.160
after SELECT and execute this again. Now we only&nbsp;
have 293 rows, meaning 293 products have a cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.560
history. Let's see what happens if I add&nbsp;
the StandardCost column to this query.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:04.560
We are back up to 395 rows, meaning that there&nbsp;
are 395 distinct combinations of a product ID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.160
and a standard cost, and this also tells us&nbsp;
there are no duplicates of a product ID and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:17.360
cost since we saw 395 product IDs originally. The&nbsp;
takeaway here is that DISTINCT means a distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.920
occurrence of a combination of values for&nbsp;
all of the columns specified in a query.

